LEC01
- There is a lot of freedom within TCS 
  - Studying algorithms and complexity of Protein Folding, Ad auctions, Quantum Tomography, Cake Cutting, Perverse sheaves, Fundamental Computational Problems

- TCS is hard
  - Expect to spend a lot of time
    - Julia Robinson tried to prove a theorem for 4 days then found out it was false
  - Don't be afraid to try different things 
    - If you try enough things, you can solve the problem by combining the progress you've made from everything
  - Make constant progress
    - 1% per day for 100 days
  - Don't give up if you cannot find a way to solve problems

- Stay up to date with TCS
  - https://theory.report/
  - https://simons.berkeley.edu/videos
  - Youtube
  - FOCS/STOC/SODA/CCC
  - arXiv/ECCC
  - Papers cited by the one you're current reading

- Managing References
  - Save local copies of every paper you look at with consistent naming convention
  - Maintain a lifetime .bib file
  - Reference Management software
    - JabRef, BibDesk
  - ams.org/mrlookup

- LaTex is non-negotiable
  - Have lifetime stub .tex file and lifetime .sty file
  - Include figures when applicable
    - import png/jpg/pdf/eps files with \includegraphics{}
    - Drawing them can be done with Inkscape, TikZ
    - Powerpoint, Keynote

- Writing tips
  - Math should always be 100% correct
    - "Otherwise, you could prove that the sun is composed of butter" (Prof. Khesin)
  - Write it in a way that your readers can understand
    - Too many symbols is NOT good

- Presentations
  - Powerpoint, Keynote
    - Integrade math equations in your slides
  - https://graphics.stanford.edu/~kayvonf/misc/cleartalktips.pdf

- Do whatever you need to finish your problem (RESEARCH ONLY)
  - If it's a sequence, search on oeis.og
  - If it's terminology, go to wikipedia
  - If it's a "random" number, use Inverse symbolic calculator
  - If it's something another expert knows, ask on mathoverflow.net
  - A lot of stack exchange websites for questions (although perhaps AI can answer them nowadays)
  - Computational problems should be sent to WolframAlpha (again, AI can probably answer them nowadays)

- Problem Solving!
  - Try small, easy to compute subcases first to gain intuition
  - Although a question might have infinite constraints (e.g. everything within a range), you can usually pick a small subset of them and it would not affect the answer that much
    - You can plug the subset of points into software to solve it for you
    - Then, from the answer, you can usually deduce the correct answer (e.g. if it's 3.0000001, it's probably 3)
    - Then, you can prove that your answer works!
      - Usually easier to prove an answer than to find an answer while proving
  - Combine this with the websites above

LEC02
- Big O Notation
  - f(x) = O(g(x)) if there exists a real number C such that for all sufficiently large x, f(x) <= C*g(x)
    - I am most familiar with this as competitive programming almost exclusively cares about big O
    - Constraints make it so that the right big O time complexity should always pass even with bad constant factor
    - While higher time complexities should never pass even with a great constant factor
  - Can be used to denote an anonymous function f(x) that is negligible to the total runtime of the function
    - E.g. x^2+9x can be written as x^2 + O(x)
  - Big Omega
    - f(x) = Omega(g(x)) if there exists a real number C > 0 such that for all sufficiently large x, f(x) >= C*g(x))
  - Big Theta
    - f(x) = Theta(g(x)) if Omega(g(x)) <= f(x) <= O(g(x))
  - There exists little o and little omega, which remove the equality part from their respective functions
  - Poly
    - f(x) = poly(g(x)) if f(x) = g(x)^O(1)
  - tilde{O}
    - f(x) = tilde{O}(g(x)) if f(x) = g(x)*poly(log(g(x))
    - There also exists tilde{omega} and tilde{theta}
  - O_k
    - Used when there are more than 1 variable in the formula
    - O_k(g(x)) means that if k is treated as a constant, f(x) = O(g(x))
  - g(n) is in standard form if it is a product of the following types
    - constants
    - constant powers of ln n
    - constant powers of n
    - exponential functions
    - n^cn, where c is a real number
    - Each type is asymptomatically more than the one before it
  - f(x) is asymptotic to g(x) for suffficient large values of x if f(x)/g(x) -> 1
    - Equivalent to f(x) = g(x)*(1 +- o(1))

- Harmonic Numbers
  - Defined as the sum of the postive number reciprocals up to that integer
    - H(n) = 1 + 1/2 + 1/3 + ... + 1/n
    - Approximately ln n
  - Obtaining a resonable UPPER bound
    - 1/3 is at most 1/2, 1/5 is at most 1/4, 1/6 is at most 1/4, 1/7 is at most 1/4, etc
    - H(n) <= 1 + 1/2 + 1/2 + 1/4 + 1/4 + 1/4 + 1/4 + ... 
    - Thus, H(n) <= floor(log2(n)) + 1 => O(H(n)) = O(log n)
  - Obtaining a reasonable LOWER bound
    - 1/3 is at least 1/4, 1/5 is at least 1/8, 1/6 is at least 1/8, 1/7 is at least 1/8, etc
    - H(n) >= 1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + 1/4 + 1/8 + ... 
    - Thus, H(n) >= (1/2)floor(log2(n)) + 1 => Omega(H(n)) = Omega(log(n))
  - Since Omega(log(n)) <= H(n) <= O(log(n)), Theta(H(n)) = Theta(log(n))
  - Approximation using integrals (I do not know how to do them)
    - It is the sum of some rectangles under the graph f(x) = 1/n
    - Upper bound is equal to 1 plus the area under the curve of f(x) from 1 to n
      - Equals ln(n) + 1 
    - If we shift the rectangles rightward, we see that the lower bound is equal to the area under the curve of f(x) from 1 to n+1
      - Equals ln(n+1)
    - Thus, ln(n+1) <= H(n) <= ln(n)+1, which supports our bounds above
